  "body": "`element.insertBefore(child, reference)` and `element.appendChild(child)` are not the most convenient way to update for example a list of items. `element.setChildren(children)` would make it so much easier to work with updating children.\r\n\r\nWhat comes to frameworks / view libraries, they all have different ways to update children. Would make so much more sense to have single optimized way to do that, so that all frameworks / view libraries would benefit.\r\n\r\nHere's an example solution for updating the children elements of a parent Node:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nfunction setChildren (parent, children) {\r\n  let traverse = parent.firstChild;\r\n\r\n  // go through children and place if not in place\r\n  for (let i = 0; i < children.length; i++) {\r\n    const child = children[i];\r\n\r\n    if (traverse === child) {\r\n      // already in place\r\n      traverse = traverse.nextSibling;\r\n    } else if (traverse) {\r\n      parent.insertBefore(child, traverse);\r\n    } else {\r\n      parent.appendChild(child);\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // remove all the rest\r\n  while (traverse) {\r\n    const next = traverse.nextSibling;\r\n    parent.removeChild(traverse);\r\n    traverse = next;\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nBackwards-compatibility could be provided easily with a polyfill.",
